2468, 7731, 25, 1396, 3817, 13260, 5743, 369, 47241, 494, 279, 1788, 33539, 7731, 279, 2100, 1558, 1000, 599, 8677, 303, 18541, 211342, 45157, 45543, 11, 748, 279, 1534, 1414, 369, 45287, 18922, 11, 524, 15756, 4283, 13, 271, 623, 6380, 11, 4478, 446, 13402, 383, 1204, 1599, 9665, 10885, 513, 11330, 1518, 264, 35678, 11, 279, 8410, 2695, 314, 279, 9665, 321, 2100, 3983, 11, 321, 279, 14588, 314, 279, 2100, 1558, 725, 14835, 22188, 1440, 13, 357, 1603, 9665, 1558, 1220, 381, 4778, 321, 25284, 440, 279, 2100, 1558, 725, 7465, 11, 1345, 279, 9665, 2984, 1220, 7915, 279, 5823, 314, 24693, 2272, 37543, 33303, 13, 271, 760, 1330, 1379, 4047, 7715, 279, 4478, 446, 369, 8882, 1056, 24389, 513, 3238, 9665, 24693, 7566, 11, 3428, 1521, 279, 9665, 1558, 369, 2166, 2526, 11, 29463, 25284, 11, 466, 279, 3274, 369, 7309, 47485, 11, 321, 1496, 29997, 494, 4162, 279, 2100, 1558, 383, 4860, 22958], "content": "We need answer user's request: summarize in about 300 words for general technical audience how speculative decoding speeds up LLM inference: draft model, target verifies several tokens in one forward pass, why output distribution identical, what determines speedup, end with two most common reasons speedup smaller than hoped. Need no preamble. Need about 300 words. Let's draft around 300 words. Need ensure ends with two most common reasons. Could be exactly? \"about 300 words\". Let's count. Need final only. Let's craft ~300 words.\n\nDraft:\nSpeculative decoding accelerates large language model inference by using a smaller, faster draft model to propose a short sequence of candidate tokens, often called a draft, before the large target model is run. The draft model is cheap to execute, so it can generate several tokens sequentially in a small amount of time. The target model then takes the original prompt plus the proposed draft and performs a single forward pass over the whole sequence. Because transformer inference can process multiple positions in parallel, this one pass yields the target model\u2019s probability distribution for the first draft token, the second draft token conditioned on the first, and so on.\n\nThe key trick is verification. The target model accepts draft tokens only when they are consistent with its own distribution. For each position, it compares the draft token\u2019s probability under the target model with the probability that would have been assigned if the target model had generated that token directly. If the target model would have chosen the token with sufficient likelihood, it is accepted; otherwise, the first rejected token is replaced by a token sampled from a corrected distribution that favors the target model\u2019s preferences. This rejection-sampling step is what preserves the output distribution: every token ultimately returned is sampled from the same conditional distribution the target model would have produced in ordinary autoregressive decoding, so the final text is statistically identical, not merely similar.\n\nIn practice, speedup depends on how many draft tokens are accepted before a rejection, the relative cost of the draft and target models, and the efficiency of the target model\u2019s parallel verification pass. A good draft model should be fast and aligned with the target model\u2019s behavior, while the draft length should balance the chance of acceptance against wasted computation.\n\nThe two most common reasons the speedup is smaller than hoped are low draft acceptance rates, often because the draft model is too small, poorly aligned, or the task is highly unpredictable, and high overhead from running the target model on longer sequences", "top2": [[[1596, -0.008125565014779568], [1421, -5.577061176300049]], [[1144, -7.033372639853042e-06], [513, -12.236804008483887]], [[4087, -0.24540187418460846], [310, -1.5714592933654785]], [[1156, -0.002149267354980111], [303, -6.256507396697998]], [[579, -0.19590696692466736], [25, -2.1480839252471924]], [[1622, -0.0006366013549268246], [3296,